## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the fundamental understanding of combining data structures to achieve O(1) operations while maintaining an ordering constraint. It evaluates knowledge of hash map + linked list patterns and eviction logic commonly used in caching systems.

## Key Concepts
- HashMap for O(1) key-to-node lookups
- Doubly linked list for maintaining recency order
- Node manipulation for constant-time recency updates
- Edge case handling: missing keys, capacity limits, and duplicate operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class LRUCache {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.head = { prev: null, next: null };
    this.tail = { prev: null, next: null };
    this.head.next = this.tail;
    this.tail.prev = this.head;
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!this.cache.has(key)) return -1;
    const node = this.cache.get(key);
    this._moveToHead(node);
    return node.value;
  }

  put(key, value) {
    if (this.cache.has(key)) {
      const node = this.cache.get(key);
      node.value = value;
      this._moveToHead(node);
    } else {
      const newNode = { key, value, prev: null, next: null };
      this.cache.set(key, newNode);
      this._addToHead(newNode);
      if (this.cache.size > this.capacity) {
        this._removeTail();
      }
    }
  }

  _moveToHead(node) {
    this._removeNode(node);
    this._addToHead(node);
  }

  _addToHead(node) {
    node.prev = this.head;
    node.next = this.head.next;
    this.head.next.prev = node;
    this.head.next = node;
  }

  _removeNode(node) {
    node.prev.next = node.next;
    node.next.prev = node.prev;
  }

  _removeTail() {
    const node = this.tail.prev;
    this._removeNode(node);
    this.cache.delete(node.key);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Dynamic string editing with fast substring queries is critical in modern editors and versioned docs. A rope with an implicit treap provides randomized balance and localized updates, enabling logarithmic edits while maintaining per-substring hashes for quick comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- Implicit treap (rope) structure for sequence storage
- Subtree size and rolling hash maintenance
- Polynomial rolling hash with two moduli to reduce collisions
- Split and merge operations for positional edits
- Substring hash isolation without copying strings

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(ch) {
    this.c = ch;
    this.pr = Math.random();
    this.left = null; this.right = null;
    this.sz = 1;
    this.h1 = ch.charCodeAt(0);
    this.h2 = ch.charCodeAt(0);
  }
}
// Update size and hashes from children
function upd(n){ if(!n) return; n.sz = 1; n.h1 = n.c.charCodeAt(0); n.h2 = n.c.charCodeAt(0);
  if(n.left){ n.sz += n.left.sz; n.h1 = n.left.h1 + somePow1; n.h2 = n.left.h2 + somePow2; }
  if(n.right){ n.sz += n.right.sz; n.h1 = n.h1 + rightHash1; n.h2 = n.h2 + rightHash2; }
}
function split(t, k){ // split first k chars to a
  if(!t) return [null, null];
  if(size(t.left) >= k){ const [l, r] = split(t.left, k); t.left = r; upd(t); return [l, t]; }
  const [l, r] = split(t.right, k - size(t.left) - 1); t.right = l; upd(t); return [t, r];
}
function merge(a, b){ if(!a||!b) return a||b; if(a.pr < b.pr){ a.right = merge(a.right, b); upd(a); return a; } else { b.left = merge(a, b.left); upd(b); return b; } }
```

